  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two band-diagram scenarios at the interface between a buffer layer and a Cu(In,Ga)Se2 or CZTS absorber, highlighting how interfacial band alignment affects solar-cell performance. In both sketches, dashed lines mark the electron and hole quasi-Fermi levels, and the separation between them indicates the open-circuit voltage, VOC.\n\nThe left side shows an unfavorable case. The bands near the interface bend in a way labeled as ineffective p-to-n inversion, and a red shaded region marks possible charge recombination. Electrons and holes are brought close together near the junction, implying that carriers can recombine instead of being extracted, which would limit device efficiency and VOC.\n\nThe right side shows a more favorable interface. The band shape appears to create a barrier or field that keeps electrons and holes spatially separated and directs them toward collection. Arrows indicate improved movement of electrons in the upper band and holes in the lower band, and the text emphasizes enhanced carrier collection. Overall, the figure illustrates that better interfacial band engineering in these thin-film absorbers suppresses recombination and improves charge extraction.",
